Second change: persistAddressValidationFailure()

This method is another important place:

SendFlexRecordDto persistAddressValidationFailure(
        AddressValidationRequestDTO addressValidationRequestDTO,
        Exception e) {

    String detailMessage = e.getMessage();
    Throwable cause = e.getCause();
    String causeMessage = (cause != null) ? cause.getMessage() : null;

    SendFlexRecordDto sendFlexRecordDto =
            SendFlexRecordDto.builder()
                    .requestObject(addressValidationRequestDTO)
                    .responseObject(detailMessage + causeMessage)
                    .statusCode(FAILURE)
                    .apiCode(ServiceCommonConstants.ADDRESS_VALIDATION_ENDPOINT)
                    .build();

    return sendFlexRecordUtil.saveSendflexRecord(sendFlexRecordDto);
}

The problematic part for the story is:

.statusCode(FAILURE)

The story explicitly says:

> If we mark the errors as NULL, then the errors will not appear in the alert dashboard.



So for the expected SendFlex address-validation 4xx case, you likely want:

.statusCode(null)

rather than:

.statusCode(FAILURE)

However, I would not blindly change FAILURE to null for every exception.

Otherwise you'll hide genuine application/technical failures from the alert dashboard.


